  "response": "The IntCal20 calibration curve extends to 100,000 calibrated years before present, and it is the reach of that curve which makes it possible for calibration against IntCal20 to convert radiocarbon ages into calendar age ranges. Those ranges, not the raw laboratory values, are the quantities that can legitimately be compared with the relative ordering recovered from the ground.\n\nEvery radiocarbon determination is reported with a laboratory measurement uncertainty, and close attention to the uncertainties attached to the Layer 4 samples shows that the two charcoal determinations from that layer are statistically indistinguishable at the 95 percent level. In other words, the pair of charcoal determinations from Layer 4 cannot be separated in age with 95 percent confidence, so no argument about the internal sequence of the layer can rest upon the gap between them.\n\nBecause the law of superposition holds that lower deposits are older than overlying deposits in undisturbed sequences, that ordering is precisely what the formal chronological machinery of the discipline was constructed to exploit. Bayesian chronological models in OxCal incorporate stratigraphic ordering as prior information, and as a result outlier analysis within such a model downweights determinations inconsistent with the stratigraphic sequence. The practical consequence is that a single discordant measurement rarely overturns a carefully observed sequence; it is instead absorbed, and its influence on the final age estimate is reduced in proportion to its discordance.\n\nTaphonomic reasoning supplies the other half of the adjudication. Rodent burrowing displaces small charcoal fragments between stratigraphic units, which indicates that the dated samples were no longer in their original depositional context. At least one of two faults must then be in play, and perhaps both: either the material had left the deposit in which it was originally laid down, or the dated samples contained carbon from a source outside the dated organism's lifetime. By contrast with the vertical movement that burrowing animals produce, charcoal derived from long-dead wood yields ages older than the context in which it was deposited, an offset that arises at the moment of burning rather than afterwards. Distinguishing these two mechanisms, each of which leaves its own signature in the size, condition and distribution of the dated fragments, is what allows an excavator to decide whether a date should be rejected, corrected or allowed to revise the stratigraphic reading.\n\nAdjudication between rival chronologies begins with a clear view of what each body of evidence is capable of delivering. Radiocarbon dating measures the residual carbon-14 in organic samples, and because that residual activity remains measurable across very long intervals, the method reliably determines ages of material up to 200,000 years old. An excavator confronted with an apparent conflict therefore has no warrant for dismissing a determination simply because it points to a remote antiquity.",
